DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers Kautsky's naturalistic and evolutionary framing of
historical materialism, his engagement with Darwin and Haeckel, and the
broader question of how socialist thinkers at the Second International
integrated Darwinian biology with Marxist social theory. The key debates
concern whether Kautsky's evolutionary Marxism constitutes a form of
"social Darwinism" -- appropriating biological selection logic for social
analysis -- or whether it maintained a principled distinction between natural
and social evolution. Weikart's foundational study (1999) argues that German
socialists including Kautsky produced a distinctive "socialist Darwinism"
that selectively borrowed from Darwin while repudiating Malthusian and
competitive-selection applications. Blackledge's critique challenges this
framing as caricature, arguing that Kautsky's early evolutionary framework
was more sophisticated and dialectically aware than the "evolutionist"
label suggests.

A second set of debates concerns the Haeckelian monist tradition: Kautsky
was influenced by Ernst Haeckel's "monism" -- the view that all natural and
social processes obey unified evolutionary laws -- which shaped his ethics
(Ethics and the Materialist Conception of History, 1906) and his
Vermehrung und Entwicklung (1910). The Weir and Stewart/Hossfeld/Levit
papers illuminate how Haeckelian monism operated as a bridge between
naturalistic Darwinism and socialist ethics. Recent scholarship (Gronow
2016, Rowley 2024) situates Kautsky's naturalism within the formation of
Second International Marxism and its contested relation to orthodox
scientific categories.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
Kautsky's philosophy of history cannot be understood without grasping his
naturalistic commitments -- the claim that social development follows laws
analogous to (though not identical with) natural selection. This domain
provides the conceptual vocabulary and scholarly debate for assessing whether
Kautsky's determinism is "biologistic" or genuinely materialist, and how his
position compares to contemporaries like Plekhanov, Bogdanov, and Spencer.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Scholarship specifically on Kautsky's Vermehrung und Entwicklung (1910) as
a philosophical text remains thin in English-language secondary literature.
The connection between Kautsky's Darwinism and his ethics (the 1906 work)
has received less sustained attention than his political economy. Entries
marked INCOMPLETE lack publicly available abstracts.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Cross-reference with Domain 1 (general Kautsky scholarship) to situate the
evolutionary dimension within Kautsky's overall philosophy of history; the
distinction between natural and social evolution is central to assessing his
determinism and his theory of progress.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Socialist Darwinism thesis: [4 papers] - Weikart, Stack, and others argue
  German socialists produced a distinctive synthesis of Darwin and Marx that
  selectively biologized social theory.
- Critique of reductionism: [3 papers] - Blackledge, Gronow argue the
  "socialist Darwinism" label misrepresents the actual dialectical complexity
  of Kautsky's evolutionary framework.
- Haeckelian monism tradition: [3 papers] - Weir, Stewart/Hossfeld/Levit
  examine how monist naturalism shaped socialist ethics in Germany.
- Historical materialism and natural selection: [2 papers] - Lattansi-Silveus,
  Rowley examine whether Darwinian selection logic can be coherently applied
  to social evolution.
====================================================================
}

